<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karytaina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karytaina is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, dominated by a castle on a crag above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> river. It was the seat of a barony of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, and its castle was built around 1254 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-bruyeres">Geoffrey of Bruyères</a>, hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> took it in 1320, and in the Greek War of Independence it served as a stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-kolokotronis">Theodoros Kolokotronis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karytaina.jpg" alt="Karytaina" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nochoje · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karytainaview.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karytaina stands on the slopes of the hill of Achreiovouni, some 550 m above sea level, on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> near its confluence with the Lousios, 54 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and 20 km from Megalopoli. The site is often identified with the ancient city of Brenthe, but few remains survive from before the Frankish conquest of about 1205. The name is of uncertain origin: it may come from a Slavic root, be a corruption of Gortyna, or derive from the Greek karydion, walnut.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Karytaina was one of the largest baronies of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and of special strategic importance, controlling the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skorta">Skorta</a> region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> ravine, the main route between the Arcadian plateau and the plains of Elis. It belonged to the Briel or Bruyères family. Its third baron, Geoffrey, repeatedly defied Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a>. After his death in 1275 the barony gradually reverted to the princes and was later held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-villehardouin">Isabella of Villehardouin</a> and her daughter Margaret of Savoy, while the Byzantine Greeks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> pressed it until they took it.</p><p>Karytaina came under Ottoman control in 1460. After Venetian rule from 1687 to 1715 the castle was abandoned, but the town grew as the centre of a kaza and a market for wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and carpets; François Pouqueville counted 130 villages with 28,170 inhabitants in the district, 3,000 of them in Karytaina. Taken by the rebels in 1821, it earned the name Bastion of 1821, and in 1826 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-kolokotronis">Theodoros Kolokotronis</a> used it as a base against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-of-egypt">Ibrahim Pasha of Egypt</a> and a shelter for women and children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Karytaina is a protected traditional settlement, part of the municipality of Megalopoli since 2011, and its townscape was shown on the Greek 5,000-drachma banknote of 1984–2001. The castle has a triangular wall over 110 m long on its long sides, repaired by Kolokotronis, who added a small church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. A five-arched bridge of 50 m across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> dates from the Frankish period and was repaired by Raoul Manuel Melikes in 1439/40, and the 11th-century Church of St. Nicholas stands in the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karytaina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geoffrey I built the great castle of Chlemoutsi, or Clermont, in about 1220–1223, reputedly with church revenues he had seized, and strongholds such as <b>Karytaina</b>, Akova and Kalavryta guarded the baronies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1320 the Byzantine governor Andronikos Asen captured Akova along with <b>Karytaina</b> and other castles of central Peloponnese, bringing the barony into what became the Despotate of the Morea.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-akova">Barony of Akova</a></li><li><blockquote>The barony of <b>Karytaina</b>, also called Skorta after its mountainous district, was created around 1209 in the Principality of Achaea and granted to Hugh of Bruyeres.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-karytaina">Barony of Karytaina</a></li></ul></section>
